  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species occurs in southernmost Myanmar, Peninsular Malaysia, Peninsular Thailand, Singapore, Indonesia (Sumatra, west and east Kalimantan), Brunei, and east Malaysia (west Sarawak and Sabah). It is also present in Tawi Tawi, Philippines.",
    "population": "<p><em>Heosemys spinosa</em> is restricted to small and isolated subpopulations in Thailand. Subpopulations in Brunei, Myanmar, Singapore, and the Philippines are small and at a low density. Subpopulations in Singapore and Brunei may be relatively secure. Data are scarce for Malaysia and Borneo. It is uncommon in Indonesia, but the populations inside protected areas are secure.<br/><br/>In Indonesia, it is regulated with a harvest quota of 250 individuals with a size limit of &lt;20 cm. There is high mortality during collection of individuals.<br/><br/>The population of this species is suspected to have been reduced by 50–80% in the last three generations based on intensive collection and habitat loss.</p>",
    "habitats": "This species inhabits flooded forests, hilly streams and lowland swamps. <br/><br/>Generation length is estimated at 17 years (age of first reproduction = 7, lifespan = 35 years, reproductive period = 22 years, z = 0.35). Three generations is 51 years.",
    "threats": "<p><em>Heosemys spinosa</em> is impacted by a combination of subsistence consumption, intensive collection for consumption trade, pet trade, and&#160; widespread degradation and loss of its lowland rainforest habitat from agriculture.&#160;</p>",
    "measures": "<p><em>Heosemys spinosa </em>is included in CITES Appendix II. It is protected from exploitation and trade under the Wildlife Preservation and Protection Act of 1992 in Thailand.<br/><br/>It is present in several protected areas throughout its range. Although the range in Thailand is small in comparison to the entire range here, the known protected areas in Thailand are Hala-Bala W.S. Narathiwat; Khao Pra-Bang Khram W.S., Krabi; Khao Luang N.P., Nakhon Si Thammarat; Ton Ngachang W.S., Songkhla.<br/><br/>Detailed monitoring of trade and status is urgently required.</p>",
    "use_trade": "The species is being used for local and international consumption and local and international pet trade.",
    "rationale": "This species is listed as Endangered as its population is suspected to have been reduced by 50–80% in the last three generations due to intensive collection for consumption trade, pet trade, and degradation and loss of its lowland rainforest habitat.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
